Mark deleted documents a bitmap page at a time, with their counts in the same WAL record

Applies to vchord_bm25 0.3.0 (upstream tag 0.3.0, commit c610b7ab), after 0001
and 0002.

ambulkdelete (vacuum.rs) write-locked the metapage before its loop and held it
until the loop ended. The loop visits every document id the index has
assigned. A search reads the metapage for its whole scan and an insert writes
it, so both waited for the whole pass: 3.06 s for 1,000,000 documents with
800,000 deleted. Holding the lock also held off interrupts, so a cancelled
VACUUM finished the pass before it stopped. Under autovacuum the cost-based
delay slept with the lock held.

The loop logged each deleted document's bit under its own WAL record. It wrote
doc_cnt and doc_term_cnt to the metapage once, when it released the lock. A
backend that died in between kept the bits and lost the counts. The next
VACUUM skipped the documents already marked, so their counts were never taken
off. In one run, 1,000,000 documents had 800,000 deleted and the VACUUM backend
was killed mid-pass. After the next VACUUM, doc_cnt was 419,689 against
200,000 true.

The pass now reads the metapage once and releases it. A document inserted
after that read cannot point at a tuple this VACUUM removes, so the pass stops
at the current_doc_id it read. It then works through one delete bitmap page
(65,280 document ids) at a time. First it finds the page's dead documents with
no lock held. Then it locks the metapage and that bitmap page, sets the bits
and subtracts the counts. Both pages change under one WAL record, so recovery
replays both changes or neither. A bit that is already set is not counted
again, so a VACUUM that re-runs after a crash or a cancel subtracts each
document once. The metapage is locked before the bitmap page, the same order
every other path uses. The lengths subtracted are the stored lengths 0002
made every side count. No on-disk format or SQL changes.

Measured on 1,000,000 documents with 800,000 deleted, unpatched then patched:
- a search started during the pass: 2.86 s, then 0.001 s;
- the whole VACUUM: 3.45 s, then 0.79 s;
- the WAL the VACUUM wrote: 49.2 MiB, then 6.3 MiB.

Also measured:
- Autovacuum's default cost delay (2 ms) on 2,110,000 documents with 420,000
  deleted: the longest search went from 4.15 s to 0.018 s.
- A cancel mid-pass: unpatched, VACUUM stopped after 2.69 s; patched, after
  0.003 s. Both left consistent counts.
- The VACUUM backend killed mid-pass: the patched index had doc_cnt 934,720
  after one bitmap page, consistent with its bits. After the next VACUUM,
  doc_cnt was 200,000 and doc_term_cnt was 2,000,000, both exact.

